<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yongle Emperor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1360">1360</a>–<a href="/years/1424">1424</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Yongle Emperor was the third emperor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming Empire</a>, reigning from 1402 to 1424 after seizing the throne from his nephew. He moved the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, began construction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/forbidden-city">Forbidden City</a> and campaigned against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>. Under his reign maritime commerce reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a> through the voyages of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zheng-he">Zheng He</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yongle-emperor.jpg" alt="Yongle Emperor" width="289" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_assis_de_l%27empereur_Ming_Chengzu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Zhu Di was born on 2 May 1360, the fourth son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hongwu-emperor">Zhu Yuanzhang</a>, who in 1368 founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a> as the Hongwu Emperor. On 22 April 1370 he was made Prince of Yan, with his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> at Beiping, the present <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, and in April 1380 he moved there. In the 1380s and 1390s he proved a skilled military leader. After the Hongwu Emperor's death, the new Jianwen Emperor's advisors set out to limit the power of the princes, removing five of them, and regarded Zhu Di as the most dangerous.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 1399 Zhu Di rebelled in the civil war known as the Jingnan campaign. He began with 100,000 soldiers and only the area around Beiping, but his troops were of higher quality. He ascended the throne on 17 July 1402 and took the era name Yongle. He erased the Jianwen Emperor's reign from the record, and tens of thousands of people were purged, among them Huang Zicheng and Qi Tai, who were put to death with their families and followers.</p><p>He made Beiping, renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> in 1403, a second capital, and its construction between 1407 and 1420 employed hundreds of thousands of workers. He had the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-canal">Grand Canal</a> rebuilt to feed the capital and the northern armies, finishing in 1415. He sponsored the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yongle-encyclopedia">Yongle Encyclopedia</a>, which employed about two thousand scholars. Unlike his father, he avoided frequent purges. He led five campaigns into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>, which failed to secure the northern border, and annexed Đại Việt in 1407 as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiaozhi-province">Jiaozhi Province</a>. In 1405 he appointed the eunuch <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zheng-he">Zheng He</a> admiral of a fleet whose voyages reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hormuz">Hormuz</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 1 April 1424 the emperor set out on his last campaign into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>, which failed. He died on 12 August 1424 at Yumuchuan, and private records suggest that a series of strokes killed him. He was buried in the Chang Mausoleum, the first of the Ming tombs north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, and his son Zhu Gaochi succeeded him as the Hongxi Emperor. Historians such as Chan Hok-lam, Edward Dreyer and Frederick Mote have described his reign as the second founding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yongle Emperor” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1414 envoys from Bengal presented a giraffe from East Africa to the <b>Yongle Emperor</b> in China, where it was hailed as a qilin, a mythical creature of good omen.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bengal-sultanate">Bengal Sultanate · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1407 the armies of the <b>Ming Yongle Emperor</b> overthrew the short-lived Hồ dynasty and annexed Đại Việt as the province of Jiaozhi, imposing heavy taxes and labour levies and carrying books and craftsmen off to China.</blockquote><a href="/empire/later-le-dynasty">Later Le dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The chief enemy was Ayutthaya, whose ambitions drove the king to China in 1418 to seek protection, and the <b>Yongle Emperor</b> warned the Siamese ruler in October 1419.</blockquote><a href="/empire/malacca-sultanate">Malacca Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Domestic markets thrived, and in the early 15th century the <b>Yongle Emperor</b> sponsored Zheng He's voyages to Southeast Asia, India and East Africa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ming-empire">Ming Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Chinese records show that in 1417 three Sulu rulers, among them Paduka Batara, led a large mission to the Ming court of the <b>Yongle Emperor</b>; Paduka Batara died on the return journey and was buried at Dezhou in Shandong, where his tomb still stands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sulu-sultanate">Sulu Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>It could also be won by war, as the <b>Yongle Emperor</b> took it from his nephew in 1402, and lost by capture: when the Oirats seized the Zhengtong Emperor in 1449 his brother took the throne, and he won it back only by a coup in 1457.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ming-china">Social Hierarchy of Ming China</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inner-mongolia">Inner Mongolia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=32.06,118.78&amp;year=1401" aria-label="Nanjing on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7606 997 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Yongle Emperor"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8056 1267h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8056 1267h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=32.06,118.78&amp;year=1401">What was here in 1401? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
